export const body = "<p>Traversal mechanics establish a relationship between players and the environment in which they're navigating. Other than the usual run and jump, some games even grant players the ability to glide, climb, or dash. Among those mechanics, there is one that rarely appears, but excites every time it does. Here, we talk about wall running.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/89a9cd9487d4ce50a79e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/89a9cd9487d4ce50a79e.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"718\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>Compared to a jump, wall running can cover farther distance—as far as the respective affordance allows. That means, with this mechanic in place, level designers can add larger pitfalls and wider gaps between safe grounds. Besides, if vertical wall running is possible, the game can present platforming challenges that occur in both axes, rather than just horizontally.</p>\n\n<p>Like every other mechanic, wall running prompts game designers to build a rule set for compatible interactions. Which surface is run-able, and which is not? How many directions can the player wall run? These questions lead us to two types of wall running: unlimited and conditional.</p>\n\n<p>Unlimited wall running means the player can run on most, if not all, vertical surfaces in the game. The quintessential example of this category is the <em>Prince of Persia</em>. The Prince can run upward or horizontally to reposition himself in combat or overcome platforming challenges. Since the whole game is designed around wall running, vertical surfaces are made flat and sturdy-looking, so there’s no visual guesswork.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/65b44ee409c1128ef789.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/65b44ee409c1128ef789.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"644\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/1c5db487e72892900d9a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/1c5db487e72892900d9a.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"579\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 3\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><em>The broken wall precludes the wall running ability.</em></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/e4c7829de8cdd9d8b833.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/e4c7829de8cdd9d8b833.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"573\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 4\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><em>Run-able walls are lined with horizontal patterns which double as leading lines.</em></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/e5ee16d1fe20ef3ab08e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/e5ee16d1fe20ef3ab08e.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"647\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 5\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>The second type, conditional, limits wall running to specific objects in the game, often denoted by a UI element or a signifier. <em>Star Wars Jedi Fallen Order</em> is a recent example. Cal Kestis, the main character, can only perform this action on certain standing surfaces. This restrained approach shifts the focus of wall running to solely traversal, so there’s no combat encounter where the Padawan can take advantage of nearby walls to get out of harm way.</p>\n\n<p>However, that doesn’t mean conditional wall running is worse than the unlimited counterpart. In <em>Kingdom Hearts 3</em>, wall running is also restricted to standing surfaces with a flashing indicator (a UI element). But unlike Cal Kestis, Sora gets to flex this ability during regular combat encounters and boss fights, as the arenas usually contain those flashing walls.</p>\n\n<p>For example, in the first level, Sora has to cross a blazing trench by bounding on a nearby wall. As he crosses the distance, some flying darklings are spawned, intercepting his progress. The result is a combat scenario in which the player has to maintain their footing on the side wall while trying to fend off these pesky enemies.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/bb708f2b81f4c51a787f.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/bb708f2b81f4c51a787f.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"647\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 6\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/164b5e305236bfa8f69c.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/164b5e305236bfa8f69c.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"646\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 7\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/e2296e33cce7ff068a35.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/e2296e33cce7ff068a35.webp\" width=\"823\" height=\"456\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 8\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/d088ac4fed7f45c09d0b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/d088ac4fed7f45c09d0b.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"645\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 9\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>To conclude this article, we'll take a look at <em>Titanfall</em> <em>2 </em>to see how wall running complement with other mechanics to offer memorable gameplay scenarios. In the \"Effect and Cause\" mission, players are granted a time-travel device which enables them to switch between two timelines at the press of a button. During this level, there is a sequence in which players have to swap the timelines constantly to latch on to the walls as they cross a gap.</p>\n\n<p>In the next mission, \"The Beacon,\" players are equipped with a special gun that can electrify the machines in the environments. Later on in this level, they'll have to use this engineering tool to activate flipping panels, turning them into walls. There are plenty of other compelling situations that require players to make full use of wall running. So if you want to see how far you can push this traversal mechanic, <em>Titanfall 2</em> is a strong recommendation.</p>";
